+#+TITLE: lazy-jail
+
+plan-report notes:
+- ~jails/instances~ has an example of a similar approach that I've
+  developed, but is based more along long-running jails managed via
+  =service jail=. Refer to it for ideas when doing the gap
+  analysis. Don't treat it as spec to be merge in here - the spec
+  needs to be self-contained. But it will bring up questions to be
+  addressed in the first version of the report.
+
+spec:
+- CLI utility for managing jails from source trees
+- intent is to check in the jail config to produce a dev jail for a
+  given repo
+- unlike ~jail.conf.d~, bastille, etc the jail config is not
+  centralized
+- jail implementation does go to a known location though,
+  e.g. ~zroot/lj/instances~ etc
+- interface
+  - =lj start <jail>=
+  - =lj stop <jail>=
+  - =lj login <jail> [user]=
+    - with no =[user]= arg, logs in as the default user defined in the
+      config file
+  - =lj list= - list running jails
+
+implementation notes:
+- write info jail =meta= subkeys to track information. This ensures
+  that we can query the lj-managed jails without having to run it from
+  a dir with a specific config.
+
+questions:
+- is this actually a useful approach? maybe we want to keep something
+  like the =jails/instances= approach after all. and just rely on
+  operator usage to know something like =doas jexec -l my-jail login
+  -f my-user=. or potentially =lj= is a really thin layer over =jexec=
+  and =service jail= commands. I kind of like the idea of not having a
+  totally distinct interface, where we can still use the basic tools
+  we know and love. Then =lj= just provides some convenience
+  functions.
